nd " [:a {:href "/athletes/68745"} "Dorothy Tyler"] " cleared that height, and failed at the next height, 1.70 (5-7) metres. As Coachman had cleared the height in her first try, she was awarded the gold ahead of Tyler. This was unlucky for the British woman, who had also won a silver medal (as Dorothy Odam) in " [:a {:href "/results/58457"} "1936"] ". Back then, she had cleared the winning height in fewer attempts, but the rules in Berlin called for a jump-off, which she lost. Coachman’s victory was notable as she was the first black woman to win an Olympic gold medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Start Order"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Best Height Cleared"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.61 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.64 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.66 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.68 m"] [:th {} "Attempts at 1.70 m"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "755"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/77790"} "Alice Coachman"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "1.68"] [:td {} "o"] [:td {} "xo"] [:td {} "xo"] [:td {} "o"] [:td {} "xxx"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "750"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/68745"} "Dorothy Tyler"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "1.68"] [:td {} "o"] [:td {} "xxo"] [:td {} "o"] [:td {} "xo"] [:td {} "xxx"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:c